# dsh command reference

Generated from the command tree by `dsh catalog commands`. Do not edit by hand.

## dsh

```text
Usage: dsh [options] [profile|command...]

The DeepSeek harness: launch a profile or run a fixed command.

Launcher options come before the profile or command name. Every word after a
profile name, or after --profile <name>, belongs to the profile's app, --help included.

Commands:
  help        Show the help of a command or a profile
  version     Print the version of this build
  catalog     Print the generated catalogs of this build
  completion  Print a shell completion script
  secret      Manage the stored secrets
  sandbox     Show how commands are confined on this machine
  config      Preview and check the composed configuration
  status      Print the hosts running on this home
  crash       Print the path of the newest crash report

Profiles:
  web          Start the host and open the web UI
  headless     Run one task without a UI and print the answer
  sdk          Serve the SDK protocol on standard input and output
  sdk-minimal  Serve the SDK protocol with the minimal agent preset
  acp          Serve the Agent Client Protocol on standard input and output

Options:
      --home <dir>      Harness home (default $DSH_HOME, then ~/.dsh)
      --profile <name>  Profile to launch; every later word goes to its app
      --layer <file>    Add a config layer above the user layer (repeatable)
      --safe            Start with optional bundles and plugins off
  -V, --version         Print the version and exit
  -h, --help            Show this help

Examples:
  dsh web
      Start the host and open the web UI
  dsh --home ./h --layer extra.yml headless "run the tests" --json
  dsh --profile sdk
      How programs start the SDK protocol
```

## dsh help

```text
Usage: dsh help [options] [command...]

Show the help of a command or a profile

Arguments:
  command  A command path or a profile name

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  dsh help catalog commands
```

## dsh version

```text
Usage: dsh version [options]

Print the version of this build

Options:
      --json  Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help  Show this help
```

## dsh catalog

```text
Usage: dsh catalog [options] <command>...

Print the generated catalogs of this build

Commands:
  commands  Print the command reference

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help
```

## dsh catalog commands

```text
Usage: dsh catalog commands [options]

Print the command reference

Options:
      --json  Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  dsh catalog commands > reference.md
```

## dsh completion

```text
Usage: dsh completion [options] <shell>

Print a shell completion script

Arguments:
  shell  bash, zsh, fish, powershell

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  dsh completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/dsh
  dsh completion powershell | Out-String | Invoke-Expression
```

## dsh secret

```text
Usage: dsh secret [options] <command>...

Manage the stored secrets

Commands:
  set  Store a secret read from standard input

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help
```

## dsh secret set

```text
Usage: dsh secret set [options] <name>

Store a secret read from standard input

The value is read from standard input, without one trailing line end; it is
never an argument and never printed. <name> is OWNER_NAME or secret://owner/name.

Arguments:
  name  The secret's name, such as DEEPSEEK_API_KEY

Options:
      --json  Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  printf %s "$KEY" | dsh secret set DEEPSEEK_API_KEY
  Get-Content key.txt | dsh secret set secret://deepseek/api-key
```

## dsh sandbox

```text
Usage: dsh sandbox [options] <command>...

Show how commands are confined on this machine

Commands:
  status  Print the confinement a command gets now

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help
```

## dsh sandbox status

```text
Usage: dsh sandbox status [options]

Print the confinement a command gets now

Probes the sandbox backends and prints Full, Partial or Unconfined, the
backend, the effects it does not govern and the exposures. Writes nothing to the home.

Options:
      --json  Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help  Show this help
```

## dsh config

```text
Usage: dsh config [options] <command>...

Preview and check the composed configuration

Commands:
  show      Print a profile's composed tree and the layer of each value
  validate  Check the configuration of one or every profile

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help
```

## dsh config show

```text
Usage: dsh config show [options] [profile]

Print a profile's composed tree and the layer of each value

Composes the profile's layers as a launch would and prints every entry and
value with the layer that supplied it, then the issues. Starts no component
and writes nothing to the home.

Arguments:
  profile  The profile, as an argument instead of --profile

Options:
      --profile <name>           The profile whose composition is read
      --defaults                 Leave the profile layer out
      --format <text|yaml|json>  Output form; yaml is one loadable layer document
      --json                     Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help                     Show this help

Examples:
  dsh config show --profile headless
  dsh config show headless --format yaml > tree.yml
  dsh --home ./h config show web --defaults --json
```

## dsh config validate

```text
Usage: dsh config validate [options] [file...]

Check the configuration of one or every profile

Checks the composition of the profile, or of every built-in profile, with
each <file> as a layer above the user layer. An issue exits 78 and names its
file, line and field. Starts no component and writes nothing to the home.

Arguments:
  file  A layer file to check above the user layer

Options:
      --profile <name>  The profile whose composition is read
      --json            Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help            Show this help

Examples:
  dsh config validate
  dsh config validate --profile web extra.yml
```

## dsh status

```text
Usage: dsh status [options]

Print the hosts running on this home

Reads the runtime records and asks each host for its status. A host that
exited uncleanly is named on standard error with its crash report and --safe.
No host running exits 4.

Options:
      --json  Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  dsh status
  dsh --home ./h status --json
```

## dsh crash

```text
Usage: dsh crash [options]

Print the path of the newest crash report

Writes the report of every host that exited uncleanly, then prints the path
of the newest report under reports/. No report exits 5.

Options:
      --json  Print the result as one JSON value
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  dsh crash
```

## Profiles

| Profile | Bundles | Summary |
|---|---|---|
| `web` | admin, core, base, web | Start the host and open the web UI |
| `headless` | admin, core, base, headless | Run one task without a UI and print the answer |
| `sdk` | admin, core, base, sdk | Serve the SDK protocol on standard input and output |
| `sdk-minimal` | admin, core, minimal, sdk | Serve the SDK protocol with the minimal agent preset |
| `acp` | admin, core, base, acp | Serve the Agent Client Protocol on standard input and output |

## Exit statuses

| Status | Name | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | ok | Success; also help and version |
| 1 | failed | The thing asked for ran and failed |
| 3 | denied | Denied by policy, a guard or a person |
| 4 | unavailable | Unavailable: no host, no endpoint, a capability that is missing |
| 5 | invalid | Invalid input or not found: an unknown session, tool or profile |
| 6 | busy | Busy or conflict |
| 7 | limit | A limit or a timeout ended the work |
| 8 | cancelled | Cancelled, or the outcome is unknown |
| 64 | usage | Usage error: unknown command or flag, a broken rule between flags, a missing argument |
| 70 | fatal | Fatal error with a report written |
| 78 | config | The configuration cannot be composed, or a required entry failed to load |
| 124 | forced | Forced exit: the exit deadline passed during shutdown |
| 125 | stalled | A stdio client stopped reading |
| 130 | interrupted | Interrupted by Ctrl-C |

## Reserved words

No profile may be called: `__complete`, `__completeNoDesc`, `catalog`, `completion`, `config`, `crash`, `help`, `sandbox`, `sandbox-launch`, `secret`, `ssh-helper`, `status`, `version`.
